! DUN72B.DBG - Phase 72's debugger session on DUNGEON.EXE, round 2
! (README.md): round 1's EXAMINEs, whose %X prefixes VMS's debugger
! refused. The radix is set to hexadecimal first (FORTRAN's default is
! decimal), so every address here is hex, and each starts with a digit.
!
SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL
SHOW RADIX
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION .PC
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION
!
! The startup stub, the first transfer address: a jump to the old
! SYS$IMGSTA.
EXAMINE/WORD 1A433
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A435
!
! The old system service vector in S0: SYS$IMGSTA (^X168), SYS$NUMTIM
! (^X1B8, the one service DUNGEON calls there), and the first entry,
! each as its entry mask and its first two instructions; then the same
! P1 vector entries.
EXAMINE/WORD 80000168
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 8000016A
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION
EXAMINE/WORD 800001B8
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 800001BA
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION
EXAMINE/WORD 80000000
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 80000002
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION
!
! How far the old vector goes: the entries at the P1 vector's highest
! offsets (^X918, SYS$SYNCH_INT's, the last), ^X400, and ^X800.
EXAMINE/WORD 80000400
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 80000402
EXAMINE/WORD 80000800
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 80000802
EXAMINE/WORD 80000918
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 8000091A
EXAMINE/WORD 80000920
EXAMINE/WORD 80000A00
!
EXAMINE/WORD 7FFEDF68
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 7FFEDF6A
EXAMINE/WORD 7FFEDFB8
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 7FFEDFBA
!
! VMSRTL's transfer vector where DUNGEON calls it (VMSRTL_001, from
! ^X1A600), as longwords, then the entries DUNGEON uses, each as its
! entry mask and its first instruction.
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 1A600:1A83C
EXAMINE/WORD 1A600
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A602
EXAMINE/WORD 1A688
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A68A
EXAMINE/WORD 1A6A8
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A6AA
EXAMINE/WORD 1A6F8
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A6FA
EXAMINE/WORD 1A770
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A772
EXAMINE/WORD 1A778
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A77A
EXAMINE/WORD 1A830
EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION 1A832
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 1AA30:1AA3C
!
! Whether the old VMSRTL_002 (^X1AE00, 49 pages) and VMSRTL_003
! (^X21000, 2 pages) ranges are mapped, and where VMSRTL ends.
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 1AE00
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 1DE00
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 1E000
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 21000
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 21200
EXAMINE/LONGWORD/HEXADECIMAL 21400
